<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Dream Jobs – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Dream Jobs</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Skills Do I Need for a Future Career?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow detailed oral instructions in class. Interactive: Interpret and relay information in pair and group discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow instructions from a career planning workshop (e.g., "I should study computer science and learn programming languages.")
            Can interpret key details in a class discussion about future jobs and relay that information accurately during pair or group work (e.g., "They said that jobs in AI will need skills like data analysis and programming.").
            Can identify key points from a group discussion on career goals, taking notes and summarizing information (e.g., "What career paths might emerge due to automation, and what skills are required?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key concepts and vocabulary.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of diverse future jobs (e.g., AI specialist, remote worker, environmental engineer, virtual reality designer). Students work in small groups and brainstorm possible job titles and the general *skills* they think these *jobs* might *require* in the *future*. The teacher collects initial ideas on the board, introducing the theme "What Skills Do I Need for a Future Career?"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words crucial for the lesson: *future*, *skills*, *demand*, *automation*, *remote work*, *AI (artificial intelligence)*, *adaptability*, *evolve*. The teacher emphasizes stress in multisyllabic words like "au-to-ma-tion" and "ad-ap-ta-bi-li-ty". The teacher models a simple sentence using future simple and a modal: "In the *future*, many *jobs* *will require* strong *adaptability*."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'in *demand*' mean for a *job*? (Is it a job many people want, or a job that many companies need?)"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a job involves 'remote work,' where do you do your work? (In an office, or from home/another location?)"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher checks understanding by asking students to give examples of skills that are currently 'in *demand*' and clarifies any doubts about the new vocabulary.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Dream Jobs".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio clip (approx. 2-3 minutes) of a brief excerpt from a career planning workshop discussion focusing on *future jobs* and *skills* needed due to *automation* and *AI*. The audio includes predictions using the future simple (e.g., "Many new *jobs* *will emerge*"), future continuous (e.g., "People *will be working* with *AI* more closely"), and modals for possibilities (e.g., "*Jobs may require* advanced *digital skills*").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Can interpret key details in a class discussion about *future jobs* and relay that information accurately during pair or group work.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question (First Listen):</b> The teacher plays the audio. Students listen and identify the main topic of the discussion. (e.g., "What was the workshop mainly about: past careers, current jobs, or *future jobs* and *skills*?")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Comprehension (Second Listen):</b> The teacher provides a True/False handout. Students listen again and mark statements as True or False based on specific details about *skills* and *job predictions* mentioned in the audio. Examples: "True/False: The speakers predict that *remote work* *will disappear*." "True/False: They say *adaptability* *will be* a key *skill*."</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening, target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Future simple to predict *jobs* (e.g., "Many new *jobs will emerge*."), Future continuous for long-term goals (e.g., "In ten years, people *will be working* with advanced *technology*."), Modals for possibilities (e.g., "*Jobs may require* new *adaptability skills*."). Vocabulary: *jobs*, *skills*, *future*, *demand*, *automation*, *remote work*, *AI (artificial intelligence)*, *adaptability*, *evolve*, *require*.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listen and Fill):</b> The teacher plays specific sentences from the audio clip (or similar, simplified sentences) that contain gaps for target vocabulary or grammar. Students listen carefully and fill in the blanks on a worksheet. (e.g., "The discussion suggests that ______ (AI) *will change* many *industries*." "Many *skills* *will* ______ (evolve) over time.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Listen and Classify):</b> Students listen to short statements (teacher-read or from audio) about *future job skills*. They classify these *skills* into categories like 'Technical *Skills*', 'Soft *Skills*', or 'Digital *Skills*' on a T-chart. (e.g., Teacher says: "Critical thinking *will be* crucial." Students write "Critical thinking" under 'Soft *Skills*'. Teacher says: "Programming *will be* highly *in-demand*." Students write "Programming" under 'Technical *Skills*').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listen and Relay):</b> In pairs, students take turns summarizing one key point they heard from the workshop discussion to their partner, focusing on using *future simple* or *modals*. The partner listens actively and verifies the information. (e.g., Student A: "The speaker said that *automation* *may replace* some repetitive *jobs*." Student B: "Yes, and they also said that *adaptability* *will be* a very important *skill*.") This helps them practice interpreting and relaying information.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Dream Jobs".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group Discussion – "Our Future *Workforce*".</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in groups of 3-4. Each student identifies one *dream job* for themselves. They then take turns describing their *dream job* and predicting what *skills* *will be required* for it in 10-15 years, considering themes like *automation*, *remote work*, and *AI*. Other group members listen, ask clarifying questions (e.g., "What *skills* *will you be developing* for that *career*?", "Do you think *AI may change* that *job* significantly?"), and offer advice or alternative perspectives using target grammar and vocabulary. They practice adapting their tone (sociolinguistic competence) when asking questions or giving suggestions to peers. For example, "You *should definitely* consider learning data analysis for that *field*."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in discussing *future jobs* and *skills*, collaborative interaction, and accurate interpretation of peers' ideas. Students should actively listen to each other's predictions and advice, then use the information to refine their own understanding or to build upon it during the discussion. They should aim to identify 2-3 key predictions or required *skills* from their peers' input.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on students' ability to follow instructions, interpret information, and relay key details during the group discussion.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group designates a "reporter" (students take turns for future lessons). The reporter listens to their group's final discussion summary and then orally relays 2-3 main *future job* predictions and associated *skills* discussed by their group to the class. The class listens and takes brief notes.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher checklist for reporters):</b></p>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of relayed information (e.g., accurately summarizes group's key points).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., *automation*, *AI*, *adaptability*, *in-demand*).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target grammar (e.g., *future simple*, *modals* for predictions).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially of multisyllabic vocabulary).</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an individual exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new *skill* you heard today that *will be important* for *future jobs*?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did you understand the discussions about *future careers* and *skills* today? (1-5 scale, 5 being perfectly clear)"</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection for continuous improvement.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new insights did you gain today about the *skills* you *will need* for your *dream job* in the *future*?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively were you able to understand and share information about *future careers* with your classmates?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson, especially regarding the listening activities and the introduction of new vocabulary? Student engagement during group discussion seemed high, and most students used the target vocabulary related to *future jobs* and *skills*.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled to relay detailed information accurately. For the next lesson (Reading), I *will provide* a graphic organizer to help them identify and categorize key information from the text more easily, building on the vocabulary introduced today. I *will also review* the pronunciation of "automation" and "artificial intelligence" in the next warm-up.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students *will read* an article about how *technology* and *automation* *are changing* the *workforce* to further consolidate the core vocabulary and concepts introduced today, practicing identifying similar predictions and *skills* in written text.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
    </tr>
</table>